<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sargon of Akkad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2334–2279 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/24th-century-bce">24th century BCE</a> · Also known as Sargon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sargon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, whose reign is conventionally dated from c. 2334 BCE. Rising from obscure origins, he conquered the Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> and created what is often called the first empire in history, ruling from the city of Akkad. Accounts of the Akkadian Empire credit him with a standing army and imperial administration, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a> record his campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad.jpg" alt="Sargon of Akkad" width="297" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ALFGRN · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_of_Akkad_on_his_victory_stele.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sargon's reign is poorly understood, since few commemorative inscriptions can be reliably dated to it, most of them known from later copies, and few archives survive from his time. Like the other kings of his dynasty he does not name his father in his inscriptions; the Sumerian Sargon Legend, written long after his death, calls his father La'ipum. He rose as ruler of the small city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, which lay in the shadow of its powerful neighbour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, though the circumstances of his rise remain unclear. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a> was a high priestess of the moon god at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> who composed hymns, among them the Exaltation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a>, that were used for centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sargon's reign began in the last phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>, when Lower Mesopotamia was divided among several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> and the idea of unifying it was emerging. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugal-zage-si">Lugal-zage-si</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> had brought the south, the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, under his control. According to datable sources it took Sargon three battles to defeat Lugal-zage-si and his allies and take Uruk, whose walls were razed. The captured king was led to the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>. The date of the conquest is debated, and it is placed around 2300 BCE.</p><p>Sargon also led expeditions beyond Lower Mesopotamia, east towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and west towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, though he did not incorporate those lands. Their purpose seems to have been glory, riches, control of trade routes and the restraint of rival powers. His inscriptions summarise his successes as 34 battles. One mentions that he fed 5,400 men at his table each day, usually understood as soldiers bound personally to him, and it names the nine regiments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>. The former kingdoms became provinces under governors, and Akkad remained the capital. His scribes began to write inscriptions in Akkadian as well as Sumerian, and the image of the king changed from a cultic and administrative official to a war hero.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The dates of Sargon's reign are uncertain; they are often estimated at 2334 to 2279 BCE, and other proposals exist. After his death his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manishtushu">Manishtushu</a> succeeded him, in an order that is unknown, and Rimush suppressed revolts in the southern cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> followed, and then Shar-kali-sharri, whose reign ended in the first half of the 22nd century BCE with the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>.</p><p>Sargon became the archetype of the conquering ruler. Stories of his birth, rise and campaigns were still being composed in the 8th century BCE, and Assyrian and Babylonian kings saw themselves as heirs of his empire. The Neo-Babylonian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> excavated the palaces of Sargon and his successors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sargon of Akkad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sargon</b>'s daughter Enheduanna, high priestess of the moon god at Ur, is often described as the first named author in recorded history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sargon of Akkad</b> later claimed that ships from Dilmun, Magan and Meluhha moored at the quay of his capital Agade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sargon of Akkad</b> listed Ebla among lands given to him by the god Dagan, and Naram-Sin later claimed to have conquered Ebla, so either may have been responsible, though some scholars argue that Mari took revenge first.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Lagash was on the losing side: Urukagina was defeated by Lugalzagesi, first as ruler of Umma and then of Uruk, and about 2300 BCE <b>Sargon of Akkad</b> 'laid waste' the territory from Lagash to the sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The second city was destroyed around 2300 BCE, probably by <b>Sargon of Akkad</b>, who claimed to have been given Mari by the god Dagan, or by his successors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sargon of Akkad</b>'s conquest met resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Azupiranu. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
